{
  "id": "COMPOUND:26744 - PROTEIN:4976",
  "verbalization": "Hex-4-enoic acid is implicated in the pathogenesis of HT-29, a human colon cancer cell line, through a series of mechanistically linked targets. The evidence suggests that Hex-4-enoic acid acts as a target for multiple compounds, all of which ultimately target A2058, a protein that is a target of several structurally diverse molecules. These molecules, including (2S,3R,4R,5E,7S)-3,4,7-trihydroxy-2-propyl-2,3,4,7,8,9-hexahydrooxecin-10-one, 5-[2-(2,4-Dimethoxyphenyl)ethyl]benzene-1,3-diol, and (15aS)-13,14,15,15a-Tetrahydro-3-methoxy-5H,11H-pyrrolo(2,1-c)quinazolino(3,2-a)(1,4)benzodiazepine-5,11-dione, all share a common mode of action that ultimately leads to targeting HT-29. The integration of these pathways suggests that Hex-4-enoic acid may exert its biological effects through a mechanism that involves the modulation of a shared target, A2058, which is a critical component in the cellular processes of HT-29. The consistent presence of this target across multiple compounds and its direct interaction with HT-29 implies a potential role for Hex-4-enoic acid in the regulation of HT-29 cell behavior, though further experimental validation is required to confirm the precise mechanism of action.",
